IN THE SUPREME COURT OF INDIA
CASE NO: Civil Appeal No. 3631 of 2022
CITATION: (2021) 2 SCC 1
YEAR: 2021

CORAM / BENCH:
Hon'ble Judges: N.V. Ramana, Sanjiv Khanna, Krishna Murari

PARTIES:
Vidya Drolia vs Durga Trading Corporation

STATUTES & ACTS REFERRED:
Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996

KEY PROVISIONS & SECTIONS:
Section 8 Arbitration Act, Section 11 Arbitration Act

LEGAL KEYWORDS & TOPICS:
arbitrability of disputes, four-fold test, tenancy disputes, prima facie review

================================================================================
                    JUDGMENT SUMMARY & RATIO DECIDENDI
================================================================================

Laid down the definitive four-fold test to determine when subject matter of a dispute is non-arbitrable, overruling earlier precedents on landlord-tenant matters.

1. ISSUES CONSIDERED:
   The court addressed questions of legal interpretation under Section 8 Arbitration Act, Section 11 Arbitration Act.

2. ANALYSIS & PRECEDENT CONTEXT:
   Having examined the statutory scheme, fundamental principles of jurisprudence,
   and constitutional safeguards, the Court reiterated the necessity of strict
   compliance with statutory procedure and evidentiary standards.

3. HELD & BINDING RATIO:
   The principle formulated in this judgment constitutes binding judicial precedent
   within its territorial and appellate jurisdiction.

